# TODO - Self-modification state-graph optimizer

## Objective

Model executable Malbolge regions as versioned state-transition graphs whose
nodes capture only semantically relevant code/data state. Derive mathematically
verified reductions that collapse equivalent mutation histories, eliminate
redundant encryption/update work, hoist invariant crazy/rotate computations, and
identify regions safe for direct native execution. Express the equivalence and
reduction rules in `.tex` and validate each admitted rewrite against executable
VM evidence.


**Current implementation.**

- `state_graph.rs` establishes a conservative exact-state baseline for classic
  interpreter-authority execution before any state reduction is trusted.
- Exact identity confirms profile, registers, full deterministic input and
  cursor, committed output, termination, and all 59,049 memory words. A
  deterministic FNV digest only selects a bucket; it never authorizes a merge.
- The forced-collision fixture replaces the digest with constant zero and proves
  unequal input states remain separate. Identical replays deduplicate exact
  nodes/edges, and specification-comparison mode is outside the admitted baseline.
- `profile.rs` consumes validated `ProfileMachineState` values directly and
  extends exact collision-safe identity to current 14-trit checkpoints. Full
  4,782,969-word replay deduplicates, and forced digest collision keeps unequal
  current checkpoints separate. This is correctness evidence, not a storage or
  throughput claim.
- A 15-sample post-commit benchmark measures the full current baseline at
  7.19 ms median snapshot clone, 26.24 ms prepared insert, and 30.76 ms exact
  replay on the recorded host. This rejects full checkpoint copy/hash as the
  expected per-step production representation while retaining it as oracle.
- Complete before/after memory witnesses across all instruction families in
  classic and current profiles establish `|Delta M| <= 2` for every requested
  step, including actual two-cell crazy/rotate and zero-cell halt/rejection
  boundaries. This opens persistent/delta memory as the next research slice.
- `MemoryDelta`/`ProfileMemoryDelta` now come from the real step engines and
  contain only actual final changed cells. The same full-memory fixtures require
  exact `(address,before,after)` equality between trace deltas and independently
  reconstructed memory differences for all classic/current instruction families.
- `memory.rs` now represents current memory as one shared complete root plus
  exact trace patches. Every patch validates `before`, empty deltas add no
  depth,
  and stepwise materialization matches the runtime's complete 4,782,969-word
  checkpoint after every traced step. Performance/compaction remains open.
- Post-commit depth evidence keeps latest-hit reads near 18 ns through depth
  4096, while root misses rise to 20751.17 ns/op. A high-depth fit is
  about 5.20 ns per traversed patch. A compaction-only lower-bound model
  still costs about 10.93 microseconds
  per step at its one-miss optimum, so a bounded-read persistent index is next.
- `index.rs` implements that next candidate as a four-level 64-way persistent
  radix over one shared root. Current traced checkpoints reconstruct exactly,
  4096 distinct overrides preserve both indexed and root reads, forged `before`
  values fail closed, and the explicit 24-bit capacity contains current 14-trit
  addresses. Performance remains unclaimed until post-commit measurement.
- Two labelled equations define the exact projection and collision-safe merge
  criterion and are registered in the machine-checked correspondence manifest.
- The first reduced future key drops only consumed input-prefix contents while
  retaining the exact cursor and remaining suffix. A `cbO` two-input fixture
  exhausts all 256 first-byte values and confirms convergence before/after the
  common future halt.
- Already-terminated states have a second admitted key containing only profile,
  committed output, and termination reason; varied memory/register/input
  fixtures
  converge and live machines fail closed outside this domain.
- Wider-profile radix geometry, broader mutation-history collapse,
  code-generation integration, and end-to-end performance claims remain active
  research and
  must be falsified independently against the exact baseline.
- Post-commit radix evidence at `ec459d0` keeps latest/root reads near 20--25
  ns through 4096 distinct overrides; depth-4096 root fallback is
  20.70 ns versus
  24118.75 ns for the linked chain. One new
  indexed override remains near 1.16
  microseconds at depth 4096. The radix is promoted as the current-profile
  memory
  candidate; exact incremental identity/dedup is now the blocker.
- `state.rs` now binds incremental state identity to one immutable
  profile/input/root lineage, evolves state exclusively from public traces, and
  materializes every current step back to the exact runtime checkpoint. Digest
  is bucket-only: forced constant collisions keep distinct states separate and
  exact replay deduplicates. Independently allocated roots fail closed instead
  of triggering complete-root comparison. Post-commit performance is next.
- Post-commit identity evidence at `f317f3e` measures incremental trace apply at
  923.78 ns/op, new-state observation at
  535.82 ns/op, and exact replay at
  406.67 ns/op. Complete-checkpoint insert/replay
  are 26.29/30.48 ms
  in the same run. Incremental identity is promoted; append-only output storage
  is now the remaining obvious history-sized state update.
- `output.rs` replaces complete-output `Vec` copying with a shared immutable
  prefix plus one tail node per committed byte while preserving exact output in
  the state key. Independent equal/different branches compare correctly, a
  65,536-byte history materializes exactly, and iterative destruction fixes the
  stack overflow discovered by that long-history fixture. Performance is next.
- Post-commit output evidence at `1d229d0` keeps persistent append near
  108--145 ns from empty through 256 KiB histories, while complete-vector
  clone/push grows to 20.83 microseconds at 256 KiB (about 144x). Persistent
  output is promoted; the next research boundary is native-region safety/guards.
- `region.rs` now supplies the first verifier-admitted shortcut class:
  untrusted exact-state certificates are normatively re-executed and must match
  exact exit/outcome/traces before promotion. Runtime reuse requires exact entry
  equality; one real entry mutation invalidates the guard, tampered claims fail,
  and rejected transitions never promote. Dependency/read-set guard reduction is
  the next native-region research boundary.
- Post-commit region evidence at `f16785d` measures exact guard hit/miss at
  55.51/59.09 ns and normative verifier
  replay at 9.09 ms. Exact guard latency is already
  cheap; dependency/read-set reduction is pursued to broaden safe reuse across
  future-irrelevant memory differences.
- `ProfileStepTrace::memory_reads` now records real transition-engine fetch,
  optional data, and optional encryption reads (at most three operations/step).
  Every current instruction family plus rejected jump encryption has exact
  role/address/value fixtures, enabling read-before-write region live-in guards
  without duplicating opcode semantics in research code.
- Verified regions now derive read-before-write memory live-ins exclusively from
  `ProfileStepTrace::memory_reads` and `memory_delta`. Exact non-memory state
  plus
  those live-ins admits an irrelevant-memory variant that exact state equality
  rejects; verified effects preserve that irrelevant memory and match direct VM
  execution exactly. Changing a live-in fails closed.
- Post-commit dependency-region evidence at `1988f14` measures exact/dependency
  guard hits at 60.57/106.88 ns, dependency miss at 72.22 ns,
  verified shortcut application at 6.88 microseconds, and a
  prepared direct normative region run at 889.60 microseconds. The
  shortcut is about 129.36x faster on the recorded host. Guard reduction is
  promoted for broader safe reuse; native code artifact integration is next.
- `execute_or_deopt()` now fixes the tier-selection semantics before native
  emission: passing dependency guards use verified effects; guard misses run the
  normative VM for the same verified budget and rebuild the original incremental
  lineage from real traces. Valid fallback, shortcut, and typed-rejection cases
  all match their normative baselines exactly.
- `artifact.rs` now defines portable effect IR v3 as an explicitly untrusted
  artifact. Independent admission reprojects profile identity, live-ins, budget,
  outcome, and every compact state effect from the verified region. Tampering
  any field fails; admitted shortcut/deopt results equal the verified-region
  baseline. Production `execution/ir/` now owns effect IR v3; host-code
  artifacts remain open.

## Acceptance

- The research identifies the minimal future-relevant state and observational
  equivalence relation, then proves/test-validates every graph merge or
  mutation-history collapse before using it for native execution.
- Definitions state domains and assumptions precisely; executable code cannot
  claim a mathematical reduction outside those stated preconditions.
- The work states a falsifiable question or hypothesis, an explicit baseline,
  and an observation that would reject or materially weaken the proposed
  technique before performance conclusions are accepted.
- If executable algorithm research is required, the stable ID is mirrored under
  `docs/research/algorithms/<id>/` and `algorithms/<id>/`; ordinary product
  engineering is not forced into that mirror.
- Every correctness-relevant equation or equivalence used by implementation has
  explicit domain assumptions and a traceable executable correspondence check.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `math/`, `docs/research/algorithms/`,
  `algorithms/`, `tests/mathematics/`.
- Required evidence: `.tex` definitions/derivations plus exhaustive,
